## Overview

Bachelor's thesis at Aarhus University, 2021, with two other students. The question we set out to answer: can music streaming work in a modern multi-person smart home with no pauses, no interrupted songs, and instant speaker handover as people move between rooms?

## What we built

A working system, not a mockup. One Raspberry Pi 4 per room, Bluetooth RSSI for indoor positioning, a tracking service we wrote in Python 3, and Spotify Connect through Raspotify for playback. Spotify Group Session handled multiple listeners in the same room.

Walk from the kitchen to the living room and the music follows you, mid-song, without touching a phone.

## What we measured

The interesting part of the thesis is that we measured the thing instead of asserting it worked.

- **2,077 timed handover samples.** Mean 0.46 s, SD 0.58, range 0.1 to 10.8 s. Most switches land near half a second.
- **Benchmarked against the alternative.** Home Assistant took 30 seconds to 4 minutes for the same handover on the same setup.
- **A 65,000-entry RSSI study** to derive the room-detection thresholds. A Python script collected roughly 31 minutes of data per position, across two rooms and multiple positions.
- **A deliberately hard test environment.** A 63 m² apartment with thin walls, chosen because signal bleed between rooms made the problem harder, not easier. Doors closed for every cross-room measurement, both devices rebooted before every run.

## Design decisions

The technical choices were product choices, and the thesis argues them as such.

**Bluetooth classic over BLE and Wi-Fi.** We tried BLE first and dropped it on hardware availability inside the timebox. Bluetooth classic measured more precise than Wi-Fi-only positioning in our own testing. We also wrote down the cost: modern phones scramble MAC addresses for unpaired devices, so a user has to pair before the system can see them.

**Extra sensors rejected on product grounds, not technical ones.** PIR, camera, laser and ultrasonic were all evaluated and dropped. Each needed visible wiring, which made the system clunky to install and ugly in a home. A usability call that overrode the technically better option.

**Build versus buy, decided on two stated axes:** time investment and modifiability. We built tracking from scratch because we needed control over it, and handed playback and multi-user to Spotify because we did not.

**Opt-in over opt-out for shared playback.** Auto-joining people to a group session would have been more automated. It would also mean walking into a room hijacks what someone else is listening to. We chose user control over automation.

## What it did not do

Worth saying plainly, because the thesis says it plainly.

One Raspberry Pi per room caps positional accuracy, and false positives between adjacent rooms were hard to eliminate. There was no UI: registering a device meant editing the Python source, so a non-programmer could not set it up. The named next steps were better tracking accuracy and a real setup flow.
